WEBVTT

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:06.620
Each token leaves behind a key and a value. Here, we're looking at one attention head.

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:15.410
As decoding continues, a new key-value pair joins the cache. The earlier vectors stay put.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:22.880
The next query reuses these vectors to attend to the available context. Keep the past, and reuse it.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:33.073
How much memory should we reserve? Here, each request gets twelve slots, before we know its final length.

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:39.500
Instead, divide the cache into blocks of four. Allocate just the blocks the tokens need.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:48.359
The same fourteen tokens now use twenty slots, instead of thirty-six. Only each request's final block has unused space.

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:56.400
Logical order can stay the same, even when physical locations differ. A block table connects the two.

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:02.100
Token six, divided by four: block one, offset two.

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:09.660
Table entry one points to physical block P two. Read slot two. It's still logical token six.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:19.860
The query compares with every valid key, across all of these physical blocks. The addresses change; the mathematics stays.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:28.920
Softmax normalizes all scores together. A physical block boundary never creates a separate denominator.

00:01:29.410 --> 00:01:35.962
These weights mix the value vectors. Paging changes storage, while preserving the attention calculation.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:42.130
Two blocks hold six tokens. Append token six to the existing tail.

00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:46.580
Token seven fills the block, without changing the table.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:53.000
Token eight crosses a boundary. Allocate a free block, and add its address to the table.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:59.420
When the request finishes, its unshared blocks return to the pool, ready for reuse.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:07.012
Two continuations share a prefix. Reference counts track the requests using each block.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:12.540
Before A writes, copy the shared tail block, and update only A's table.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:17.820
A writes privately. B is unchanged; the full prefix stays shared.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:22.460
Logical continuity. Physical freedom. That's paged attention.
